{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>וְאֶת הַמִּשְׁכָּן תַּעֲשֶׂה עֶשֶׂר יְרִיעֹת — And the Mishkan you shall make of ten curtains.</b> It should have said 'וְעָשִׂיתָ מִשְׁכָּן' ('And you shall make a Mishkan'), in the manner that it says 'וְעָשִׂיתָ שֻׁלְחָן' ('And you shall make a table') and 'עָשִׂיתָ מְנֹרַת זָהָב' ('You shall make a golden Menorah'). But since 'Mishkan' is named for 'וְשָׁכַנְתִּי בְּתוֹכָם' ('And I shall dwell among them' — Exodus 25:8), it is not fitting to use the language of 'making' with regard to His Shekhinah, blessed be He, for this matter may be attributed to HaShem alone, as it says (Leviticus 26:11) 'וְנָתַתִּי מִשְׁכָּנִי בְּתוֹכְכֶם' ('And I shall place My dwelling in your midst'). He did not wish to mention the language of making except regarding the ten curtains, and not regarding His Mishkan, blessed be He. And this is its interpretation: 'And the Mishkan' concerning which I have already said 'וְשָׁכַנְתִּי בְּתוֹכָם' — that will come about through your making ten curtains, etc. \\n\\nHowever, regarding the plan of its construction, all the commentators agreed that it was built upon the plan of three worlds, to indicate that the world endures upon the foundation of this structure. And therefore it was made of ten curtains corresponding to the worlds in their totality, which were created by ten utterances, and all of them were suspended until the sixth day of Sivan, on condition that Israel accept the Ten Commandments. Those of the creation of the world are called 'utterances' (מַאֲמָרוֹת), for every utterance is soft speech, and so all the works of creation were created in their desired form, in the shapes they chose for themselves without coercion. But the commandments (דִּבְּרוֹת) that the Master of heaven and earth spoke to us were harsh, saying: 'If you accept the Torah, well and good; and if not, there shall be your grave.' And this is what it says (Psalms 29:4) 'קוֹל ה׳ בַּכֹּחַ' ('The voice of YHWH is in power'), for He coerced them by force into accepting the Torah. Therefore the length of a single curtain was twenty-eight cubits, equal to the numerical value of 'כֹּחַ' ('power'), and as it is written (Psalms 111:6) 'כֹּחַ מַעֲשָׂיו הִגִּיד לְעַמּוֹ' ('The power of His works He has declared to His people') — for the Tablets are the work of Elohim, and He declared to them things hard as sinews, namely those punishments, according to Rashi's interpretation, and that is 'כֹּחַ' ('power'). \\n\\nAnd the curtains were five opposite five, corresponding to the Ten Commandments, which were five commandments opposite five. Now the first five commandments are between man and the Omnipresent, blessed be He, and the latter five commandments are between man and his fellow. And so Ba'al ha-Turim explained as well that they corresponded to the Ten Commandments, five opposite five, and the fifty clasps corresponded to the fifty gates of understanding." "And I add onyx from my own, to say that through them a person can unite with the upper beings, because all of them were entrusted to Moses lacking one, as it says (Psalms 8:6) 'וַתְּחַסְּרֵהוּ מְעַט מֵאֱלֹהִים' ('Yet You have made him lack a little from Elohim') — meaning that if he were complete in all of them he would not be lacking from Elohim. And to allude to this it says 'חֲמִשִּׁים לוּלָאוֹת תַּעֲשֶׂה' ('You shall make fifty loops') etc., 'וְעָשִׂיתָ חֲמִשִּׁים קַרְסֵי זָהָב' ('And you shall make fifty clasps of gold') etc., 'וְהָיָה הַמִּשְׁכָּן אֶחָד' ('and the Mishkan shall be one') — for through the number fifty, the upper ones and the lower ones shall become one. \\n\\nFor those five curtains that correspond to the first commandments contain an allusion to the upper beings who sit first in the kingdom of heaven, and those five curtains that correspond to the latter commandments correspond to the lower ones, and the number fifty joins them, as stated. \\n\\nYou will understand and perceive, that the parokhet-veil which separated between the Holy and the Holy of Holies was placed beneath the clasps, as it says (Exodus 26:33) 'וְנָתַתָּה אֶת הַפָּרֹכֶת תַּחַת הַקְּרָסִים' ('And you shall place the parokhet beneath the clasps'). And the reason is that the parokhet and the clasps indicate one and the same matter, for the parokhet separated between the place designated for His Shekhinah, blessed be He, which is called Holy of Holies, and the Tent wherein were the sacred vessels that allude to the successes of human beings. For the Menorah is eighteen handbreadths high like the stature of an average man, and the Table is the place from which they are nourished. And lest you say that in no way is there a place where a person may cleave to YHWH his Elohim — therefore these clasps come in that place to join them, to teach that through a person's attaining insight through the fifty gates of understanding, a person cleaves to his Elohim. \\n\\nAnd from here you may learn as well that the five curtains that covered the Holy of Holies correspond to the first five commandments, for they are for the honor of the Omnipresent, blessed be He, and the five curtains that covered the Tent of Meeting correspond to the latter five commandments which pertain to created beings alone, and the joining of the fifty clasps indicates that the number fifty joins them. And these matters are clear, venerable, and correct." "Now there is room for me to explain 'five opposite five' in accordance with what our Sages of blessed memory said in tractate Berakhot (10a): those five 'Bless, my soul' (בָּרְכִי נַפְשִׁי) that David said correspond to the soul, which is compared in five respects to HaShem, blessed be He, and their joining and cleaving is through the fifty gates of understanding, as stated. \\n\\nAnd look to the right and see, for everything the Holy One, blessed be He, created has in it some intermediary that joins the two extremes. And all the commentators agreed to say that man — on the side of his having within him a divine portion from above, joined to the physical body — behold, he is the intermediary that merges upper and lower, and makes peace between the host on high and the host below, so that there be no opposition between them. \\n\\nAnd just as man is an intermediary, so too man's formation [comes] from the place of which it is said 'מִזְבַּח אֲדָמָה תַּעֲשֶׂה לִּי' (Exodus 20:21) ('An altar of earth you shall make for Me'), for the Temple as well is the center of the world and the place that merges upper and lower, for there the Holy One, blessed be He, made for Himself a dwelling among the lower ones — like the vision that YHWH showed Jacob in the ladder that was set on the earth and its head reaching the heavens, and the middle of its slope corresponding to the Temple. For the ladder joined earth to heaven through the Temple in the middle, for that place uniquely designated to be a dwelling for upper and lower is the intermediary that merges them. \\n\\nAnd therefore we find in this sacred structure matters that merge the extremes, and it is the matter of which we have spoken: that the fifty clasps joined the curtains, five of which were placed over the Holy of Holies, the portion of the upper beings, and five of them placed over the Holy, which contains the portion of the lower beings, and the clasps merge them. And this is what our Sages of blessed memory said (Yerushalmi Megillah 1:12): 'The clasps appeared in the Mishkan like stars in the firmament' — for the stars are the intermediary world between the upper world and the lower world. And just as the stars are intermediary and they too have a share in the merging, to merge upper to lower, so too the clasps in the Mishkan came with this intention. \\n\\nAnd it is written (Daniel 12:3) 'וּמַצְדִּיקֵי הָרַבִּים כַּכּוֹכָבִים לְעוֹלָם וָעֶד' ('And those who bring the multitudes to righteousness — like the stars, forever and ever'), and those who bring the multitudes to righteousness are the righteous, the foundations of the world, who make peace between the host on high and the host below — that is, 'multitudes' (רַבִּים), for they are intermediary like these stars, to exclude one who does not engage in Torah: he has no share of the upper beings at all and is not intermediary; therefore he is not included in 'those who bring the multitudes to righteousness' (מַצְדִּיקֵי הָרַבִּים), for the expression 'bring to righteousness' (מַצְדִּיקֵי) is said of one who nullifies the opposition between the two extremes." "And for this reason yet another miracle was performed here that indicates this matter, namely the middle bar that would extend from one end to the other, gripping both extremes and joining them. \\n\\nAnd why specifically the middle bar? But surely, so that we may learn from this that every middle and intermediary joins the extremes, like the Temple, and the righteous, and the stars, as stated. And in the Midrash (Tanḥuma, Terumah 9) they said that Jacob brought with him the middle bar to Egypt, etc. \\n\\nAnd perhaps he learned this from the vision of the ladder, as explained above (Genesis 28:12). \\n\\nAnd furthermore: that the Temple stands in the middle of the world and sends flow to the entire world from one end to the other — therefore this miracle was performed in it through the middle bar."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {0 "The verse address Exodus 25:8 for 'וְשָׁכַנְתִּי בְּתוֹכָם' is inferred from context; the Kli Yakar does not explicitly cite it here.", 1 "The phrase 'I add onyx from my own' (מוֹסִיף נוֹפֶךְ מִשֶּׁלִּי) is an idiom meaning 'I add a further adornment of my own' but is rendered literally to preserve the jewel imagery."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:23:56.590621715Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}